// A raw pointer as a CONTAINER ELEMENT. Reported by the first external project (KB-10): declaring an
// `InlineArray<UnsafePtr>#(1)` was enough to fail the build — the natural spelling for a
// `WGPUPipelineLayoutDescriptor`'s array of bind-group layout handles.
//
// The declaration alone was the trigger, with no `view()` call anywhere near it: `InlineArray`
// materialises its `view()` for every instantiation, and `View<T>.swap` calls the generic `relocate<T>`.
// Inference then asked `isConcreteTypeArg` whether `T = UnsafePtr` was something it could specialize
// against, and got NO — a raw pointer is neither a primitive nor a registered type, because it is one
// machine word whatever it points at and so nothing ever registers a class for it. The diagnostic pointed
// at `lib/std/collections/view.kama`, a file the author did not write.
//
// Both spellings, because the typed one failed differently and was NOT a workaround: the untyped form
// reported "no instantiation was resolved" and the typed form "cannot infer generic type parameter 'T'".
import { std::collections::View };

unsafe fn int32 raw() {
    UnsafePtr p = null;
    InlineArray<UnsafePtr>#(3) a = [p, p, p];
    borrow a.view() as v { v.swap(i: 0, j: 2); return cast<int32>(v.length()); }
}
unsafe fn int32 typed() {
    UnsafePtr<uint8> q = null;
    InlineArray<UnsafePtr<uint8>>#(2) b = [q, q];
    borrow b.view() as v { return cast<int32>(v.length()); }
}
fn int32 main() { return raw() * 10 + typed(); }   // 30 + 2
